	"1294": {
		"cId": "1294",
		"name": {
			"nominative": "Holy Wonderworkers and Unmercenaries Cosmas and Damian of Mesopotamia, and their mother, Saint Theodotia (3<sup>rd</sup> C)",
			"short": "Unmercenaries"
		},
		"scriptures": [
			{
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "apostol",
				"reading": "I Cor_12:27-13:8a",
				"pericope": "153"
			},
			{
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "gospel",
				"reading": "Mt_10:1,5-8",
				"pericope": "34 (mid.)"
			}
		],
		"hymns": [
			{
				"kind": "troparion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "0",
				"body": "<p>O Holy Unmercenaries and Wonderworkers Cosmas and Damian, visit our infirmities. Freely have you received; freely grant to us.</p>"
			},
			{
				"kind": "kontakion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "2",
				"body": "<p>Having received the grace of healings, you extend health to those in need, O Physicians [and] Most Glorious Wonderworkers. But by your visitation, destroy the insolence of the enemies, healing the world through your wonders.</p>"
			}
		]
	},
	"1295": {
		"cId": "1295",
		"name": {
			"nominative": "Martyrs and Unmercenaries Cosmas and Damian in Cilicia (Arabia), with their brothers the Martyrs Leontius, Anthymus and Eutropius († 287 or 303)",
			"short": "Martyrs and"
		},
		"scriptures": [],
		"hymns": []
	},
	"1296": {
		"cId": "1296",
		"name": {
			"nominative": "St. Triphyllius, Bishop of Leucosia (Nicosia) in Cyprus († c. 370)",
			"short": "St. Triphyllius"
		},
		"scriptures": [],
		"hymns": [
			{
				"kind": "troparion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "4",
				"body": "<p>The truth of things revealed thee to thy flock as a rule of faith, * a model of meekness and a teacher of abstinence * wherefore thou hast attained the heights through humility * and riches through poverty. ** O hierarch Triphyllius our father, entreat Christ God that our souls be saved.</p>"
			},
			{
				"kind": "kontakion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "0",
				"body": "<p>Receiving the purity of virginity through the excellence of thy life, O Triphyllius, * thou wast the first hierarch of Leucosia * and wast shown to be its evangelizer and instructor in the knowledge of God. ** Wherefore, with joy we cry out to thee: Rejoice, O adornment of hierarchs!</p>"
			}
		]
	},
	"1297": {
		"cId": "1297",
		"name": {
			"nominative": "Venerables Anna († 826) and her son John in Constantinople (9<sup>th</sup> C)",
			"short": "Venerables Anna"
		},
		"scriptures": [],
		"hymns": []
	},
	"1298": {
		"cId": "1298",
		"name": {
			"nominative": "Holy Unmercenaries Cosmas and Damian, Martyrs at Rome († 284)",
			"short": "Unmercenaries"
		},
		"scriptures": [
			{
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "apostol",
				"reading": "I Cor_12:27-13:8a",
				"pericope": "153"
			},
			{
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "gospel",
				"reading": "Mt_10:1,5-8",
				"pericope": "34 (mid.)"
			}
		],
		"hymns": [
			{
				"kind": "troparion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "0",
				"body": "<p>O holy unmercenaries and wonderworkers Cosmas and Damian, visit our infirmities. ** Freely have ye received, freely give unto us.</p>"
			},
			{
				"kind": "kontakion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "2",
				"body": "<p>Having received the grace of healings, * ye extend health to those in need, * O all-glorious physicians and wonderworkers. * By your visitation cast down the audacity of the enemy, ** healing the world with miracles.</p>"
			}
		]
	},
	"1301": {
		"cId": "1301",
		"name": {
			"nominative": "Saint Methodius, Patriarch of Constantinople († 846)",
			"short": "Saint Methodius"
		},
		"scriptures": [],
		"hymns": [
			{
				"kind": "troparion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "4",
				"body": "<p>The truth of things revealed thee to thy flock as a rule of faith, * a model of meekness and a teacher of abstinence * wherefore thou hast attained the heights through humility * and riches through poverty. ** O hierarch Methodius our father, entreat Christ God that our souls be saved.</p>"
			},
			{
				"kind": "kontakion",
				"service": "liturgy",
				"type": "1",
				"tone": "2",
				"body": "<p>Thou didst struggle on earth like an incorporeal being, O Methodius, * and hast inherited the heavens, * as one who explained the veneration of icons to the ends of the earth; * for subjected all the more to labors and pangs, ** thou didst not cease boldly to denounce those who cast aside the icons of Christ.</p>"
			}
		]
	},
